New `core.gc.monitor` module to observe GC allocations and deallocations at runtime

The new `core.gc.monitor` module allows tooling such as memory profilers
to receive a callback for every GC allocation and for every block freed,
including blocks reclaimed during a collection, as well as after each
completed collection.

---
import core.gc.monitor;

pragma(crt_constructor) void registerMonitors()
{
    setGCMonitor(&onAlloc, &onFree);
}

void onAlloc(void* ptr, size_t size) nothrow @nogc { /* ... */ }
void onFree(void* ptr, size_t size, bool collected) nothrow @nogc { /* ... */ }
---

The callbacks run from inside the GC — with the GC lock held, or with all
threads suspended during a collection — so they must be `nothrow @nogc`
and must not allocate or call back into the GC. Only the conservative GC
(the default) reports to the monitors at the moment.
